vector<int> nextSmaller(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    vector<int> ans(n, -1);
    stack<int> st;
    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        while (!st.empty() && st.top() >= arr[i]) {
            st.pop();
        }
        if (!st.empty()) {
            ans[i] = st.top();
        }
        st.push(arr[i]);
    }
    return ans;
};


1. Intuition
For every element, we need to find the nearest smaller element on its right.
The brute-force approach checks every element to the right, resulting in O(n²) time.
Instead, we use a monotonic increasing stack.
Since we need information from the right, we traverse the array right to left.

2. Approach
Create an answer array initialized with -1.
Create an empty stack to store elements.
Traverse the array from right to left.
While the stack is not empty and the top element is greater than or equal to the current element, pop it because it cannot be the next smaller element.
If the stack is not empty, the top of the stack is the next smaller element.
Store it in the answer array.
Push the current element onto the stack.

3. Why This Works
The stack always contains elements that are smaller than the current element.
Any element that is greater than or equal to the current element is removed because it cannot be the next smaller element.
Therefore, the top of the stack always represents the nearest smaller element on the right.

4. Algorithm
Initialize answer array with -1.
Create an empty stack.
Traverse from right to left.
While stack is not empty AND
      stack.top() >= current element:
      Pop
If stack is not empty:
      answer[i] = stack.top()
Push current element.
Return answer

Complexity
Time Complexity : O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)
